Engine: the battle's events, numbered, "CMEV" (open fix 56).

STATUS: APPLIED 30 September 2026 by work order 194 (Part B; reserved by
work order 193) — orion2re 5bd4c404 on orionlayer-local (before: 6f87c151), this
file's diff byte for byte (index lines included). Required by
tools/version_check.py since work order 194.

It was applied, from ~/orion2re on the orionlayer-local branch, with:

    patch -p1 < ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_combat_events.patch

and a rebuild. It comes back off with `git revert 5bd4c404` or `patch -R -p1`,
and a rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    The original plays every effect inside a blocking loop and tells
    nobody: ext::Tick runs only where the battle waits for input or between
    AI steps, so a client that watches snapshots sees almost none of it —
    work order 193 measured 1 beam picture of 9 and none of the warp-out.
    Data's decision of 30 September 2026 lets the engine run ahead of HD's
    animations, on the condition that HD shows no result and takes no input
    before the player has seen the animation that belongs to it. That needs
    the events themselves, in order, with their outcome.

    Seventeen one-line hooks in seven combat files call ext::Combat_Event
    with values that already exist at that point — a shot and its damage
    past the shields and absorbed (per shot, Fire_Ship_), reflected damage,
    bombs, special weapons fired, missile / torpedo / fighter launches,
    merges, hits, fighter passes and every missile's end (Kill_Missile_),
    moves (from, to, cost, teleport — recorded as the move starts, because
    Move_Ship_'s step loop writes the position per step) and turns,
    destructions with their death state, retreats, blast and plasma-web
    damage, captures and raids. The call computes nothing, draws no random
    number and never ticks; it copies into a ring of 1024 in src/ext, only
    while a battle is live (open fix 52's gate — the bombardment that
    reuses Fire_Ship_ outside a battle records nothing). Block "CMEV",
    after FBSC on ANY screen while events are unsent: the first event's
    number (from 1, across battles; a first above the client's next number
    is a gap) and each event's battle serial, kind and eight int16. Every
    state block in the same snapshot is the battle AFTER those events, so a
    client that animates plays them and then shows that state.

    Not events (read from CMBT's state instead): cloak, stasis, tractor and
    black hole on/off. Engine behaviour of its own, not changed here: in
    super-fast mode a beam never reduces a missile stack and the spherical
    animation draws random numbers only when drawn (work order 194's
    reading) — events report what the engine does either way.

    An engine without OrionLayer behaves exactly as before: the ring is ext
    state nothing in the game reads. One marker, `OrionLayer, open fix 56.`,
    on one line at every changed place.

